A reader who may read a page but not an Armature issue it names sees the key and "Not available" (chip, issue block, "Linked in Armature"). That reads like a fault, so people report it as a bug or think Armature is down. They should see that it is about their access, and what to do.

- Say it in words, on the chip's card, in the issue block and in the "Linked in Armature" list: "You may not see PROJ-12 in Armature. Ask the project's administrators in Armature for access." with a link that opens the issue in Armature (Armature then shows its own refusal or a request for access).
- Keep the cases apart, each with its own sentence:
  - no token stored: "Connect your Armature account under Your profile, Armature" (as today);
  - token refused: paste a new token (as today);
  - Armature unreachable: try again later (as today);
  - no access: the sentence above;
  - the issue no longer exists for anyone (deleted, or a key that never existed, e.g. a typo): "PROJ-12 does not exist in Armature (any more)".
- Armature answers 404 for both "missing" and "not yours", by design. Tell them apart without asking Armature to leak more: the issue was found when it was inserted (the picker only inserts what the author saw), and Stator's per-person cache or a webhook (`issue.deleted` if Armature sends it; otherwise a lookup by a reader who has access) can say an issue is gone. When Stator cannot tell, say both: "You may not see PROJ-12, or it no longer exists." Never reveal the summary or anything else Armature did not show this reader.
- The key itself is already on the page, so saying the issue exists adds nothing the reader could not know; record this in the decision.
- Issue list, chart and roadmap: when the reader may see none of the project, say so the same way instead of an empty table or "no project".
- en and de; vitest for each case; the real-Armature layer (`make test-armature`) checks Nils's sentences for RA-2 (no access) and for a deleted issue.

